WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:06.020
Hello, and welcome to your second drink of Cafe Disco.

2
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.280
Everyone, before you give this one a re-listen, I feel the need to address a fan mail flurry

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.460
we got about this episode, specifically about how I am pronouncing something in the episode.

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:18.280
Oh, yeah.

5
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:19.620
I saw that in the mail.

6
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:20.860
Yeah, you couldn't miss it.

7
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:21.020
No.

8
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.300
There was so much of it.

9
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.540
So in this episode, there's a storyline that Jim and Pam are going to secretly elope in

10
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:27.880
Ohio.

11
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.840
And I guess I said the name of the town wrong.

12
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:31.800
It happens.

13
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:32.860
Repeatedly, though.

14
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:33.140
Well.

15
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.420
Like so many times.

16
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.040
We've both done it.

17
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:41.180
I guess I'm saying Youngstown instead of Youngstown, Ohio.

18
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.240
And it really bugged people.

19
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.360
One person said it was like, quote, nails on a chalkboard.

20
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:47.740
Ooh.

21
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.560
So I guess, everyone, brace yourself, and I apologize.

22
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.460
Well, Jenna, you weren't the only person mispronouncing things.

23
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.120
We got a letter from Matthew G.

24
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.140
in Vermont who said, throughout the episode Cafe Disco,

25
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.460
Michael says Expresso twice and Espresso twice.

26
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.220
Was Espresso scripted as something Michael would say, or was that accidental?

27
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:17.740
Well, Matthew, I looked in the script, and it was scripted as Espresso, which means the

28
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.300
times he said Espresso were probably improvised.

29
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:21.800
Hmm.

30
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.280
Which tracks, though.

31
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:23.940
Tracks.

32
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:24.520
It does.

33
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.060
And then, lady, in rewatching the episode, I have a coffee mug catch that I didn't notice

34
00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:29.720
the first time.

35
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.960
At one minute and 47 seconds, Dwight is holding a mug, and I don't think I've ever seen this

36
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:36.480
mug before.

37
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.600
What is it?

38
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.160
It's not his usual mug?

39
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:39.780
No.

40
00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.140
It's a Yankees mug.

41
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.360
A Yankees mug?

42
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.060
I know.

43
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.900
A New York Yankees mug.

44
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:45.580
I know.

45
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.340
Why would he have that?

46
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.260
I don't know.

47
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:49.780
That is odd.

48
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:50.880
Good catch.

49
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.080
I wonder if it's like something rain grabbed.

50
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.360
I know, right?

51
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:55.920
I know.

52
00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:01.000
Well, something else we didn't check on the first time we broke down this episode is if

53
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.520
there were any real cafe discos out there in the world.

54
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.400
And we've uncovered several shulalas and cereal shacks.

55
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.560
But what about cafe discos?

56
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.560
What did you find?

57
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.920
Please tell me there are some.

58
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:13.980
There are.

59
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.600
But sort of.

60
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.960
The closest match is a place literally called Cafe Disco.

61
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.780
It's in Brisbane, Australia.

62
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.060
It has coffee and brunch from 7 a.m.

63
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:26.800
to 2 p.m.

64
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.940
with a Bangladesh-inspired menu.

65
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.020
And they play music.

66
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.960
But there is no dancing.

67
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:41.340
So I also found a few places, and they were doing more like disco cafes, which if you remember

68
00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.340
in the episode, Michael says is a no.

69
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.080
Kevin says like a disco cafe, and Michael's like, no.

70
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:47.540
No, no, no.

71
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.380
But I could not find anywhere where you drink coffee and dance.

72
00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:52.920
That I could not find.

73
00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.200
So that is still open.

74
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.020
And the thing I'm realizing with this rewatch is that Michael does not have the worst ideas

75
00:02:59.020 --> 00:02:59.800
for businesses.

76
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.180
I mean, Michael Scott Paper Company wasn't great, but his others are pretty good.

77
00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.900
Cafe Disco sounds like a fun place.

78
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.100
I know.

79
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:08.580
Right?

80
00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:09.960
And you know what?

81
00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.640
At our age, there is nothing I would love more than to go dancing at 1 p.m.

82
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.560
You know what I mean?

83
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:17.380
Yeah.

84
00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.500
That seems fun.

85
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.060
With a latte.

86
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:20.940
With a latte.

87
00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:21.980
All right, everyone.

88
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.120
Well, that's all we've got.

89
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.060
Please enjoy your second drink of Cafe Disco.

90
00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.220
I'm Jenna Fisher.

91
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.520
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

92
00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.800
We were on The Office together.

93
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:35.040
And we're best friends.

94
00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.860
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

95
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.240
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

96
00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.860
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

97
00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.000
We're The Office ladies.

98
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.520
Hello, everybody.

99
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.340
And Angela, can I get a good morning?

100
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:54.620
Yeah, you can.

101
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.740
Good morning, Jenna.

102
00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:56.720
Good morning, Sam.

103
00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:57.800
Good morning, Cassie.

104
00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.680
Good morning to you out there who's just starting your day and you're listening to us in your

105
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.020
car.

106
00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.300
Or good evening to my night owls who are listening maybe by the fire, maybe with a glass of wine.

107
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.060
I don't know.

108
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.840
That was a beautiful picture that you just painted.

109
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.500
Lady, guess what I did this morning?

110
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:15.240
What?

111
00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:15.920
Something new.

112
00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:16.880
I've never done it before.

113
00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:17.860
Exercise.

114
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:26.200
No.

115
00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.420
But I'm so tickled by that.

116
00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.600
I took a contrast shower.

117
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.340
I don't know what that is.

118
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.720
I didn't know what it was either.

119
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:37.660
Is it a cold shower?

120
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.860
It is an alternating hot and cold shower.

121
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.080
Why would you do that?

122
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.040
Okay, because it's supposed to have a bunch of health benefits.

123
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:45.740
And you know me.

124
00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:46.620
I'll try anything.

125
00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.180
You could have gotten that shower at my grandparents' house in their guest bathroom.

126
00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.740
Does it just automatically alternate for you?

127
00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:53.480
It used to.

128
00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.480
It's like, oh, wait, wait.

129
00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.700
Who just started the dishwasher?

130
00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.060
Don't shower.

131
00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.700
Oh, that's true at our house.

132
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.240
Don't do a load of laundry before you get in the shower.

133
00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.860
Or you could have a contrast shower.

134
00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.740
Well, here's what it's supposed to do.

135
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.880
It's supposed to improve blood flow, reduce systemic inflammation.

136
00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:14.760
It supports a connection between the brain and digestive system.

137
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.600
But it's also just supposed to, like, energize you and kind of boost your immune system.

138
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:22.560
I don't know.

139
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:23.860
It's supposed to be beneficial.

140
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:30.820
And it's sort of based on a thing that athletes do, you know, where they do a cold plunge and a hot shower and things.

141
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:32.200
Oh, my gosh.

142
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.020
I wonder if Tom Brady does contrast showers.

143
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:36.480
Tom Brady does it all.

144
00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.640
That's what I mean.

145
00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:38.360
I'm telling you.

146
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.060
He does it all.

147
00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:40.280
I should Google that.

148
00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.160
Do you think that's on the internet?

149
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.840
His water drinking is on the internet?

150
00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:45.580
Okay.

151
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:46.700
I bet it is.

152
00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:47.260
Okay.

153
00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:48.160
Well, listen.

154
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:49.240
I did it this morning.

155
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:49.660
You know what?

156
00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.020
Google Tom Brady shower.

157
00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:51.960
Oh, no.

158
00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.260
Don't Google that.

159
00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:54.760
And see what you get.

160
00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:55.360
Oh.

161
00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:55.960
Okay.

162
00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:56.500
Well.

163
00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:58.900
My husband's going to be like, why are you Googling?

164
00:05:59.140 --> 00:05:59.520
What is this search?

165
00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:01.080
They've given up on our search history.

166
00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:01.940
This is true.

167
00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:02.900
Wait, wait.

168
00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.200
Do you feel energized?

169
00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:05.860
I kind of do.

170
00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:06.640
Okay.

171
00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:07.280
I do.

172
00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:12.720
I definitely felt energized when the freezing cold water hit my body after being in warm

173
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:13.040
water.

174
00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.680
This would just be called grumpy shower in my house.

175
00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:19.840
I had to sort of sing it out during the cold part.

176
00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.860
Like when the cold water hit me, I was like, and it's cold.

177
00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:25.500
Yes, it's cold.

178
00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.800
You turned into like a ye olde musical act.

179
00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:29.180
I did.

180
00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.020
And my kids were like, what is mom doing today?

181
00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:33.520
Anyway, there you go.

182
00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.300
So that's what I did today, this morning.

183
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.780
Did you do anything special or new or different, Ange?

184
00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.560
I did like 15 push-ups.

185
00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:40.740
This morning?

186
00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:41.100
Yeah.

187
00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.260
Before we got here.

188
00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:42.540
Yeah.

189
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.780
Do you remember John Krasinski used to do that?

190
00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:45.560
What?

191
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:46.280
Yes.

192
00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.520
In the mornings, he would get to work.

193
00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.140
And before hair and makeup, he would do a bunch of push-ups and a bunch of sit-ups.

194
00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:52.880
Oh, my God.

195
00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.540
To energize himself for the morning.

196
00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:55.400
You don't remember that?

197
00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:56.020
No.

198
00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:59.620
I was always so impressed, and I was inspired, and I thought, oh, I should do that.

199
00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:02.540
I tried it one morning, and I was like, I'm not doing that again.

200
00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:04.420
Well, I didn't do it either.

201
00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:05.120
I was lying.

202
00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:07.160
I was doing a joke.

203
00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:08.260
But geez, John.

204
00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:08.860
Wow.

205
00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:09.720
I know.

206
00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:11.220
Should we get into this episode?

207
00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:12.060
Let's do it.

208
00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.220
This episode today is Cafe Disco.

209
00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:22.540
It is Season 5, Episode 27, written by Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan, and directed

210
00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:23.960
by Randall Einhorn.

211
00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:25.780
Would you like a summary?

212
00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:26.480
Yes.

213
00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:34.480
Concerned about the serious mood of his employees and just the general drab work environment at

214
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:41.400
Dunder Mifflin since Charles Minor left, Michael has decided to transform the former

215
00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:49.220
Michael Scott Paper Company into a cafe disco for socializing, dancing, and sipping espresso.

216
00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:55.000
Now, initially, nobody is interested, but it does slowly take off and ends up exceeding

217
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.220
Michael's expectations.

218
00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:04.120
Meanwhile, Pam and Jim plan to secretly elope to Youngstown, Ohio, and Dwight tries to help

219
00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:05.260
Phyllis with an injury.

220
00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:06.540
It's a lot.

221
00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:07.760
That's a lot happening.

222
00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:08.380
That's a lot.

223
00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:10.740
And yet, it's a pretty simple episode, really.

224
00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.220
Fast fact number one was a fan question from Becca D.

225
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:18.000
What was the inspiration for this

226
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:18.500
episode?

227
00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.480
We reached out to writers Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan.

228
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.340
Yes, Warren and Halstead were a fantastic writing team.

229
00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.960
I knew them for a long time from our days at the Groundlings.

230
00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.540
And some of you guys listening to this know, and some don't, but Warren and I used to be

231
00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:36.040
married, and we're good friends.

232
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.740
And it was really fun to reminisce with both of them about this episode.

233
00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:44.860
Well, here's what he said about the inspiration for this episode, which I thought was amazing.

234
00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.780
He said that he and Halstead were pretty new writers at the time.

235
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:54.780
They joined in this season, and they were tasked with coming up with an episode idea

236
00:08:54.780 --> 00:09:00.920
for what Michael Scott could do with this space that was formerly the Michael Scott Paper Company.

237
00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.280
He said that they were working in the writer's trailer with Brent Forrester,

238
00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.120
and the three of them were kind of pitching ideas like, oh, what if it's a cafe?

239
00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:09.340
What if it's a disco?

240
00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.060
And they couldn't really decide.

241
00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:15.860
He said for some reason that day, Paul Lieberstein was also working in the writer's trailer.

242
00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.420
He was working out of B.J.

243
00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:22.000
Novak's office that was sort of off of this communal area.

244
00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:24.300
So he could hear them going back and forth.

245
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:24.940
Yes, exactly.

246
00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:29.320
And Warren said that Paul just suddenly came out of the office and said,

247
00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.840
it's a cafe and a disco.

248
00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.740
Michael Scott loves two things more than anything, coffee and dancing.

249
00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:37.720
It's a cafe disco.

250
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:41.600
And then he turned around, went back into B.J.'s office, and shut the door.

251
00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:47.180
And Warren said he remembers Brent turning to them and saying, well, you heard him.

252
00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:53.080
And then the three of them just started outlining what is a cafe disco to Michael Scott.

253
00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:56.120
It feels very much like something your dad would do, you know?

254
00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.560
Like he'd come out as you were arguing and be like, it's both.

255
00:09:59.140 --> 00:09:59.920
Just do it.

256
00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:01.360
Yes, exactly.

257
00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:05.120
Well, it is more interesting being both, isn't it?

258
00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:09.440
And it's more Michael Scott that it would be a strange hybrid of two things.

259
00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:10.100
Yes.

260
00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.700
Halstead had some really great stuff to share as well.

261
00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:19.580
And he told us that they won an NAACP Image Award for Best Writing for a Comedy Series for this episode.

262
00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.000
This episode is really popular.

263
00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:28.360
When I told people that we were breaking down Cafe Disco and when we were trading emails with Randy Cordray about this one,

264
