769
00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:26.760
Kevin just can't get Lynn and the bacon smell off his mind, and he asks her out.

770
00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:30.540
I love the smell of bacon on a woman.

771
00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:33.000
Hey, Lynn, it's Kevin.

772
00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:38.440
I was thinking about going and getting a coffee at the cafe disco.

773
00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:40.780
I'm wondering if you want to go with.

774
00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:42.820
Wow.

775
00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:44.400
Yes.

776
00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:48.420
So later when you see them slow dancing, and then when they have a full-on makeout,

777
00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:52.580
it all started with this bacon scene by the elevators.

778
00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:54.520
That was amazing.

779
00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:55.760
What a find.

780
00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:56.520
I know.

781
00:33:56.780 --> 00:33:57.820
I so enjoyed it.

782
00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:02.700
Well, when Michael is kind of describing his inspiration for this disco,

783
00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:04.700
and how it's going to bring people together,

784
00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:08.180
and it's going to make people happy, and kind of break them out of their funk,

785
00:34:08.180 --> 00:34:14.860
I have to say that he is right on about disco in general.

786
00:34:15.660 --> 00:34:21.740
I was reading a bunch about disco and listening to disco music when I was prepping this episode.

787
00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:22.980
This is very you.

788
00:34:23.140 --> 00:34:23.560
I know.

789
00:34:23.700 --> 00:34:24.740
I like a theme.

790
00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:25.500
This is your Sounds of Scranton.

791
00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:26.180
It is.

792
00:34:26.340 --> 00:34:27.380
It really is.

793
00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:30.320
And here is what I found out.

794
00:34:30.340 --> 00:34:33.660
And I pulled a couple of quotes because I loved them.

795
00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:38.460
So disco in general was just a very inclusive and accepting culture.

796
00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:39.760
It was very communal.

797
00:34:39.940 --> 00:34:41.140
It was more than just music.

798
00:34:41.340 --> 00:34:43.000
It really was a cultural movement.

799
00:34:43.240 --> 00:34:48.080
And there's this book called Turn the Beat Around by author Peter Shapiro,

800
00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:50.560
and he called disco, quote,

801
00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:55.260
the embodiment of the vision of peace that the 60s yearned for.

802
00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:58.760
An article in Timeline.com went on to say,

803
00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:03.260
Disco was created by Black and Latino musicians in the early 70s

804
00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:07.540
and popularized by mostly gay DJs for a mostly gay crowd

805
00:35:07.540 --> 00:35:11.100
celebrating new freedoms earned by the gay rights movement.

806
00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:17.460
And finally, Niall Rogers, the guitarist for the hit disco band Chic, said,

807
00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:22.980
This was the world I wanted to live in, where everybody was cool with everyone.

808
00:35:23.380 --> 00:35:25.780
That was because of the music.

809
00:35:26.540 --> 00:35:27.720
That was disco.

810
00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:28.760
That was disco.

811
00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:30.960
I mean, I'm a kid of the 70s, you know.

812
00:35:31.060 --> 00:35:36.820
My mom and her friends were so excited because they got to go see Donna Summer in concert.

813
00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:37.720
Queen of disco.

814
00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:39.300
The queen of disco.

815
00:35:39.460 --> 00:35:42.400
My mom said it was a phenomenal show.

816
00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:45.480
How cool that your mom got to see her perform live.

817
00:35:45.620 --> 00:35:46.100
I know.

818
00:35:46.100 --> 00:35:48.060
I would have loved to have gone with my mom.

819
00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.220
I was too little because I love disco.

820
00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:54.960
But Angela Martin, of course, is not a fan of Michael pulling everyone off of work

821
00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:57.220
and taking them down to his disco cafe.

822
00:35:57.720 --> 00:35:58.540
Cafe disco.

823
00:35:58.820 --> 00:35:59.500
His cafe disco.

824
00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:04.740
And you'll see that Kevin is there very messily making his espresso.

825
00:36:04.980 --> 00:36:07.060
And it's because he's waiting for Lynn.

826
00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:12.220
Had you seen all of that deleted runner, you would know he's hoping Lynn will show up.

827
00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:15.320
In fact, in the shooting draft, the scene is more extended.

828
00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:17.760
And Kevin tells Michael he's waiting for Lynn.

829
00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:18.400
Wow.

830
00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:19.780
Before Angela enters.

831
00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:24.220
And this scene when I enter, I could barely get through, Jenna.

832
00:36:24.600 --> 00:36:26.840
I think you can tell I'm breaking if you watch it.

833
00:36:26.920 --> 00:36:30.860
Because whenever Steve as Michael offers Kevin the fake cookie in his hand,

834
00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:36.040
I literally, I start smiling when I talk because I'm having such a hard time not laughing.

835
00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:38.080
It's so absurd and wonderful.

836
00:36:38.340 --> 00:36:42.100
And a lot of that is Brian Baumgartner's performance.

837
00:36:42.320 --> 00:36:42.620
Yes.

838
00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:48.880
His, like, immediate belief that there's a cookie in a hand that clearly didn't have a cookie one second ago.

839
00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:49.460
Yes.

840
00:36:49.620 --> 00:36:53.300
Well, the three of us improvised the whole last couplet of dialogue.

841
00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:54.220
No way.

842
00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:54.680
Yes.

843
00:36:54.680 --> 00:36:56.920
So in the script, I mean, the script was phenomenal.

844
00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:02.460
But as we started doing it, Steve, as Michael, just kept messing with Kevin.

845
00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:05.820
He kept showing him the cookie, but then pulling it a little out of reach.

846
00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.840
So Kevin had to walk further into the room.

847
00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:10.500
And that was just stuff we were making up on the day.

848
00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:14.420
Well, in the script, it was just a little cleaner of an ending, right?

849
00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:15.260
It was a little simpler.

850
00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:17.140
It was like Michael said, Kevin, stay.

851
00:37:17.320 --> 00:37:18.360
Angela said, Kevin, come.

852
00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:20.000
And then Michael's like, Kevin, cookie.

853
00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:21.080
And then Kevin's like, what?

854
00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:21.700
There's a cookie?

855
00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:23.560
And then I drag him away.

856
00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:26.400
We just had so much fun with this fake cookie.

857
00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:31.360
We just improvised this little couplet at the end where Kevin's like, wait, is there a cookie?

858
00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:32.180
And Michael's like, mm-hmm.

859
00:37:32.360 --> 00:37:35.480
And I'm like, no, for goodness sakes, Kevin, there's no cookie.

860
00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:36.140
There's no cookie.

861
00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:36.620
Come on.

862
00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:37.740
And I kind of, like, show him.

863
00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:39.420
I'm trying to pry Michael's hand.

864
00:37:39.780 --> 00:37:41.480
And Kevin's like, I wanted a cookie.

865
00:37:41.480 --> 00:37:44.000
And then I look back at Michael, and I'm like, completely unacceptable.

866
00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:45.600
It was so fun.

867
00:37:45.740 --> 00:37:46.600
We had a blaSt.

868
00:37:47.260 --> 00:37:51.060
And somewhere out there, there's just footage of me laughing every time.

869
00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:54.180
Kevin looks at Michael's hand and is like, what, is there a cookie?

870
00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:55.460
It's so good.

871
00:37:55.980 --> 00:37:57.520
Well, now Michael is alone.

872
00:37:57.940 --> 00:37:59.880
He really wants people to come.

873
00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.100
So he is going to blast music.

874
00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:04.360
Through the vent.

875
00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:04.980
Yes.

876
00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:07.520
And people up in the bullpen can hear it.

877
00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:11.260
And everyone kind of starts, you know, moving and grooving a little bit.

878
00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:14.660
So much so that Phyllis decides she's going to go.

879
00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:16.180
Yeah, she says, to heck with it.

880
00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:21.120
But first, she's going to stop by Vance Refrigeration to invite Bob, where she runs

881
00:38:21.120 --> 00:38:25.680
into Bob's younger, yet sort of lookalike secretary.

882
00:38:26.060 --> 00:38:28.580
This is how this character was described in the script.

883
00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:34.700
As Phyllis walks in, Bob's busty secretary, Jessica, is coming out of Bob's office and

884
00:38:34.700 --> 00:38:35.960
closing the door behind her.

885
00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:38.060
She wears a low-cut top.

886
00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:40.120
Phyllis looks her up and down.

887
00:38:40.610 --> 00:38:41.380
Oh, wow.

888
00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:47.300
So it doesn't mention that she's meant to sort of be this younger version of Phyllis.

889
00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:49.960
Because they dressed her just like Phyllis dresses.

890
00:38:50.440 --> 00:38:50.800
I know.

891
00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:54.680
And I felt like even like their little noses were similar and everything.

892
00:38:54.960 --> 00:38:56.000
Their hairstyles.

893
00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:56.420
Yes.

894
00:38:56.640 --> 00:39:00.520
She's clearly like what Phyllis would think Bob would want in a younger woman.

895
00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:01.120
Yes.

896
00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:04.060
Which I thought was such a great casting choice.

897
00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:07.900
The woman who played Jessica was Alyssa Preston.

898
00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:14.480
And according to her IMDb, this job was her first television credit.

899
00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:16.140
Oh, she did so great.

900
00:39:16.460 --> 00:39:18.180
Do you remember your first television job?

901
00:39:18.380 --> 00:39:19.340
Oh, God, I do.

902
00:39:19.860 --> 00:39:20.280
What was it?

903
00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:21.280
I think I was horrible.

904
00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:22.220
What was it?

905
00:39:22.400 --> 00:39:26.540
Oh, my very first line on a television show was a TV show called Step by Step.

906
00:39:26.800 --> 00:39:29.600
The big stars were Suzanne Somers and Patrick Duffy.

907
00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:31.520
Was it a three-camera show?

908
00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:33.140
It was a multi-camera show.

909
00:39:33.340 --> 00:39:36.520
In the episode, they think their daughter has run off to elope.

910
00:39:36.520 --> 00:39:42.460
And I was cast because I was the same height and same hair color as the young girl playing

911
00:39:42.460 --> 00:39:42.980
their daughter.

912
00:39:44.080 --> 00:39:50.080
And they burst into this wedding chapel like in Vegas or somewhere.

913
00:39:50.380 --> 00:39:53.600
They burst in and I'm up there saying my vows.

914
00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:59.220
So Suzanne Somers whips me around at the altar and she goes, you're not Dana.

915
00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:01.620
And I say, no, I'm Simone.

916
00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:05.040
And thanks for ruining the best day of my life.

917
00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:06.220
Wow.

918
00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:09.820
Is that crazy that I remember that?

919
00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:11.660
And I auditioned for it.

920
00:40:11.900 --> 00:40:14.980
And it wasn't for like two months later that I shot it.

921
00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:19.580
And I said to the director that day, I said, how do you want me to do it?

922
00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:21.340
And he said, just like you did in the audition.

923
00:40:21.580 --> 00:40:23.440
I couldn't remember how I did it in the audition.

924
00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:27.000
And then when I did it, they were like, can you tone it down?

925
00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:31.820
Because I guess I was like, but thanks for ruining the best day of my life.

926
00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:33.260
Well, that's the worSt.

927
00:40:33.260 --> 00:40:39.560
When you have one or two lines on a show and there's no like entrance ramp to your performance.

928
00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:43.040
You just have to deliver this one line exactly right.

929
00:40:43.200 --> 00:40:46.200
And you're on this new set with new people.

930
00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:48.960
And there's all this just adrenaline running through you.

931
00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:50.720
Suzanne Somers, hi.

932
00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:54.580
I've watched her my whole childhood, whips me around.

933
00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:59.500
And while I was waiting to do my scene after the director gave me that note in rehearsals,

934
00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:03.120
I literally was in the wings going, thanks for ruining the best day of my life.

935
00:41:03.220 --> 00:41:05.020
Thanks for ruining the best day of my life.

936
00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:08.060
Thank you for ruining the best day.

937
00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:10.480
That's the other problem when you have one line.

938
00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:14.020
You way overanalyze it so much.

939
00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:14.920
I was horrible.

940
00:41:15.460 --> 00:41:17.240
Well, Alyssa Preston was amazing.

941
00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:18.080
She was.

942
00:41:19.500 --> 00:41:23.220
She has gone on to work steadily in television and film.

943
00:41:23.500 --> 00:41:30.620
She was in Grey's Anatomy, Modern Family, Jane the Virgin, the movie Horrible Bosses 2, No Monk, No Law and Order.

944
00:41:30.880 --> 00:41:31.320
Comedy.

945
00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:32.640
She's a comedy gal.

946
00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:33.460
Comedy gal.

947
00:41:33.740 --> 00:41:36.260
Back to the story at hand.

948
00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:43.860
Phyllis has decided to go down and start dancing with Michael while she waits for Bob to join her.

949
00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:46.300
Yeah, and she's really getting into it.

950
00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:51.380
So much so, she does kind of this pony move and throws out her back.

951
00:41:52.800 --> 00:41:58.660
That gasping, sort of bleeped mother effort that she says is not in the shooting draft.

952
00:41:58.860 --> 00:41:59.140
Really?

953
00:41:59.600 --> 00:41:59.940
Yeah.

954
00:42:00.100 --> 00:42:05.720
It says that she lets out a yell, you know, but I think that they had fun with that on the day.

955
00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:09.260
Can I just say that Phyllis is very good at fake injury?

956
00:42:09.260 --> 00:42:11.020
I so believed it.

957
00:42:11.120 --> 00:42:12.140
I was worried for her.

958
00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:12.800
I know.

959
00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:16.180
I mean, she gets her, like, whole face to turn red and stuff.

960
00:42:16.380 --> 00:42:20.760
And, like, everything she does as a person with a back injury was so right on for me.

961
00:42:21.060 --> 00:42:22.600
She's great at physical comedy.

962
00:42:22.880 --> 00:42:24.140
I've got a side story.

963
00:42:24.380 --> 00:42:24.720
What?

964
00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:25.960
I know we just had one.

965
00:42:26.120 --> 00:42:26.380
Uh-oh.

966
00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:32.340
I threw out my back once, just like Phyllis, and it was while I was talking to Christy Brinkley.

967
00:42:32.980 --> 00:42:33.420
What?

968
00:42:33.740 --> 00:42:33.980
Yeah.

969
00:42:34.580 --> 00:42:35.440
Christy Brinkley.

970
00:42:35.520 --> 00:42:37.600
How do you throw out your back while talking?

971
00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:39.100
Phyllis was dancing.

972
00:42:39.460 --> 00:42:40.540
Well, I was backstage.

973
00:42:40.540 --> 00:42:46.220
I was doing this game show, and Christy Brinkley was in the waiting room.

974
00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:50.200
She was going to be a guest on the next version of the game show, you know, the next episode.

975
00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:50.640
Sure, sure.

976
00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:55.880
I was just so excited, I mean, to maybe meet Christy Brinkley.

977
00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:57.340
Did you run up or something?

978
00:42:57.600 --> 00:43:03.240
I threw my arms out in a gesture where I was like, hi, Christy Brinkley.

979
00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:09.500
And in throwing, just gesturing my arms open, I threw out my back.

980
00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:10.660
It was like a pop.

981
00:43:10.840 --> 00:43:16.380
It felt like something popped in my back, but I was trying to hold it together because she

982
00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:19.140
smiled and she was talking with me.

983
00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:25.180
And I don't know if she could see the pain, like, washing over my eyes and my whole body.

984
00:43:25.340 --> 00:43:29.060
And like I said, you know, if Phyllis gets all red, I felt hot.

985
00:43:29.280 --> 00:43:31.060
Like, I felt that.

986
00:43:31.500 --> 00:43:33.100
And then it just seized up.

987
00:43:33.200 --> 00:43:34.280
Like, I couldn't even move.

988
00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:35.940
They brought the medic in.

989
00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:37.320
On the game show.

990
00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:37.740
Yes.

991
00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:41.440
They gave me some ice and some Advil.

992
00:43:41.440 --> 00:43:48.000
And from there, I had to get loaded in a car to go to this movie in Canada with Ben Stiller

993
00:43:48.000 --> 00:43:48.780
and Mike White.

994
00:43:49.060 --> 00:43:50.320
It was my dream job.

995
00:43:50.440 --> 00:43:51.500
My dream job.

996
00:43:51.500 --> 00:43:54.860
I'm playing Ben Stiller's wife in a Mike White movie.

997
00:43:55.120 --> 00:43:56.300
And you couldn't move.

998
00:43:56.420 --> 00:43:57.120
I couldn't move.

999
00:43:57.240 --> 00:44:00.280
All because you wanted to show Christy Brinkley a big gesture.

1000
00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:01.460
That I was excited to meet her.

1001
00:44:01.700 --> 00:44:02.020
Hey!

1002
00:44:02.660 --> 00:44:02.920
Yes!

1003
00:44:03.620 --> 00:44:09.380
When I got to the airport, they had to have a wheelchair load me onto the plane.

1004
00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:10.460
I mean, I really couldn't walk.

1005
00:44:10.780 --> 00:44:17.400
When I got to Canada, they had a doctor meet me at my hotel.

1006
00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:18.400
This is crazy.

1007
00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:21.080
And the next day, it was a driving scene.

1008
00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:24.060
My character was supposed to drive, and I couldn't do it.

1009
00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:26.760
And I was mortified.

1010
00:44:26.980 --> 00:44:28.600
I was mortified.

1011
00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:31.760
Because, of course, when everybody was like, how did you throw out your back?

1012
00:44:31.940 --> 00:44:34.160
And you were like, I just made a hugging gesture.

1013
00:44:34.420 --> 00:44:34.660
Yeah.

1014
00:44:34.860 --> 00:44:35.840
I just went, hey.

1015
00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:38.820
And then I seemed like a Looney Tune.

1016
00:44:40.220 --> 00:44:41.100
