1269
00:55:01.980 --> 00:55:08.160
Renee from Fargo, North Dakota would like to know who wrote that line, she loves it.

1270
00:55:08.160 --> 00:55:13.740
So we asked Warren and Halstead this question, and Warren said it's probably his favorite

1271
00:55:13.740 --> 00:55:15.820
question because he loves that line too.

1272
00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:22.760
He shared that he and Halstead were writing together on a short-lived show for the WB called

1273
00:55:22.760 --> 00:55:26.760
Modern Men, starring Jane Seymour and Max Greenfield.

1274
00:55:26.900 --> 00:55:31.540
And in one of the episodes, one of the guys is trying to break up with his drab girlfriend

1275
00:55:31.540 --> 00:55:34.260
played by guest actor Arden Mirren.

1276
00:55:34.380 --> 00:55:35.100
Oh, I love her.

1277
00:55:35.240 --> 00:55:35.940
She's hilarious.

1278
00:55:35.940 --> 00:55:36.740
She's so funny.

1279
00:55:36.740 --> 00:55:42.880
Warren said she was amazingly funny, and Halstead and Warren had pitched a line that she hates

1280
00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:43.340
music.

1281
00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:48.620
And what Arden's character said was, not a fan, like about music.

1282
00:55:48.900 --> 00:55:49.040
Yeah.

1283
00:55:49.180 --> 00:55:51.560
And they said that Arden killed it, and it was really funny.

1284
00:55:51.640 --> 00:55:55.880
And when they went to write this episode, they really could see Angela identifying with

1285
00:55:55.880 --> 00:55:58.960
that, and they borrowed their own line and added a little tweak.

1286
00:55:59.260 --> 00:56:04.880
I think it is such an amazing character trait for a person to just be like, I don't get

1287
00:56:04.880 --> 00:56:05.280
music.

1288
00:56:05.280 --> 00:56:06.500
Yeah, I'm not a fan.

1289
00:56:07.560 --> 00:56:09.740
I'm not a big music person myself.

1290
00:56:10.080 --> 00:56:13.880
I know this about you, Jenna, because when we went on our writer's retreat, you were

1291
00:56:13.880 --> 00:56:15.760
very concentrated on our meal plan.

1292
00:56:15.760 --> 00:56:17.300
What were we going to be eating?

1293
00:56:17.600 --> 00:56:17.840
Yes.

1294
00:56:17.840 --> 00:56:20.840
And I said something like, what about a playlist for the car ride?

1295
00:56:20.840 --> 00:56:21.900
And you're like, yeah, I don't care.

1296
00:56:22.180 --> 00:56:29.660
I don't know if this is why, but the car that I drove out to LA, it had no air conditioning

1297
00:56:29.660 --> 00:56:31.900
and no working radio.

1298
00:56:32.320 --> 00:56:33.640
Oh, my gosh.

1299
00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:37.360
You were driving in a car with no music on a road trip?

1300
00:56:37.600 --> 00:56:38.040
Yeah.

1301
00:56:38.240 --> 00:56:43.040
And I also had this car in college, and my college was three hours from my hometown.

1302
00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:48.940
And so I would often drive very, very, very long distances in silence.

1303
00:56:49.180 --> 00:56:49.820
Jenna.

1304
00:56:50.280 --> 00:56:55.920
Or I sometimes did bring like an old-fashioned cassette player.

1305
00:56:56.080 --> 00:56:57.260
And hit play, I hope.

1306
00:56:57.260 --> 00:56:59.420
Yeah, I could play off of that.

1307
00:56:59.700 --> 00:57:03.920
But I got really conditioned to driving without sound.

1308
00:57:03.920 --> 00:57:12.920
And now I listen to news or podcasts, but I just never kind of got into playlists or music.

1309
00:57:12.920 --> 00:57:13.220
Oh, my gosh.

1310
00:57:13.220 --> 00:57:15.520
I find it like, I don't know.

1311
00:57:15.800 --> 00:57:17.360
I do like music.

1312
00:57:17.580 --> 00:57:18.640
Like, I have whole albuMs.

1313
00:57:18.800 --> 00:57:20.340
I have Sounds of Scranton, you know?

1314
00:57:20.400 --> 00:57:20.580
Yeah, yeah.

1315
00:57:20.580 --> 00:57:24.680
Like, I use music a lot when I'm formulating a character, which is so interesting.

1316
00:57:24.680 --> 00:57:30.160
Because if you had to formulate the character of Jenna Fischer, you would have no music.

1317
00:57:30.420 --> 00:57:32.140
You'd have no music, but good food.

1318
00:57:33.160 --> 00:57:35.480
Yeah, I mean, I love a combo.

1319
00:57:35.680 --> 00:57:38.100
I love a playlist for a road trip.

1320
00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:41.200
But then I like to turn it off and talk, you know, because I'm chatty.

1321
00:57:41.440 --> 00:57:44.300
My mom always says the best place to talk is in the car.

1322
00:57:44.620 --> 00:57:47.280
That's why I love to do school pickup and drop off.

1323
00:57:47.380 --> 00:57:49.200
I feel like my kids are super chatty.

1324
00:57:49.380 --> 00:57:51.080
Yeah, talking about their day.

1325
00:57:51.100 --> 00:57:51.680
Right after school.

1326
00:57:51.860 --> 00:57:52.280
Yeah.

1327
00:57:52.540 --> 00:57:54.260
But my husband loves a playliSt.

1328
00:57:54.260 --> 00:57:57.000
And he makes a playlist for every road trip we take.

1329
00:57:57.100 --> 00:58:00.560
And it's really cute because the kids now are so connected to it.

1330
00:58:00.740 --> 00:58:05.260
So we were listening a lot to the Red Hot Chili Peppers when we went up to the mountains in Big Bear.

1331
00:58:05.640 --> 00:58:08.900
And Jack will say, whenever they come on now, he's like, that's Big Bear.

1332
00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:12.440
You know, my kids and Lee are very connected to music.

1333
00:58:12.440 --> 00:58:13.700
And they do the same thing.

1334
00:58:13.720 --> 00:58:15.820
And we have those same things.

1335
00:58:16.140 --> 00:58:19.320
Well, like Vampire Weekend for us was like the summer of 2019.

1336
00:58:19.420 --> 00:58:20.740
That's Vampire Weekend.

1337
00:58:20.860 --> 00:58:22.640
White Stripes, Seven Nation Army.

1338
00:58:22.960 --> 00:58:23.160
Love it.

1339
00:58:23.160 --> 00:58:27.580
And we would listen to that song as we were driving up to go skiing in Santa Fe.

1340
00:58:27.720 --> 00:58:27.980
Oh, yeah.

1341
00:58:28.100 --> 00:58:31.000
So whenever I hear that song, I just feel the snowy mountain.

1342
00:58:31.280 --> 00:58:32.460
I'm going to Wichita.

1343
00:58:32.860 --> 00:58:33.160
Yeah.

1344
00:58:33.500 --> 00:58:34.440
It's so good.

1345
00:58:34.580 --> 00:58:35.040
So good.

1346
00:58:35.660 --> 00:58:40.380
Oh, can I take us into the conference room with Dwight and Phyllis?

1347
00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:42.600
Where he's treating her like livestock?

1348
00:58:42.960 --> 00:58:46.360
And he's rubbing otter oil into her back?

1349
00:58:46.520 --> 00:58:47.620
Yeah, and giving her a carrot.

1350
00:58:47.620 --> 00:58:51.160
I thought for sure this otter oil thing was made up.

1351
00:58:51.320 --> 00:58:52.180
Is it real?

1352
00:58:52.720 --> 00:58:58.120
Lady, otter glands can be purchased.

1353
00:58:58.500 --> 00:58:59.100
Oh.

1354
00:58:59.360 --> 00:59:00.520
How do they get them?

1355
00:59:00.840 --> 00:59:02.380
Well, from dead otters.

1356
00:59:02.380 --> 00:59:02.780
Okay.

1357
00:59:03.000 --> 00:59:04.700
They take their anal glands.

1358
00:59:04.780 --> 00:59:05.340
Oh, Lord.

1359
00:59:05.340 --> 00:59:10.160
And they grind them up and they age them and then they preserve them.

1360
00:59:10.360 --> 00:59:11.660
Who thought of that?

1361
00:59:11.840 --> 00:59:14.440
Who thought it was like, save that otter's ass?

1362
00:59:14.520 --> 00:59:15.480
I don't know.

1363
00:59:15.480 --> 00:59:18.000
There's some stuff in there I need to grind up.

1364
00:59:18.160 --> 00:59:21.360
You can get one ounce for about eight bucks.

1365
00:59:22.020 --> 00:59:24.800
On another website, I saw you could get it for six.

1366
00:59:24.980 --> 00:59:27.160
So if you're in the market, shop around.

1367
00:59:27.360 --> 00:59:28.760
Well, what do people use it for?

1368
00:59:28.980 --> 00:59:30.600
That's what I wanted to know.

1369
00:59:30.880 --> 00:59:34.060
I guess you use it to lure an otter.

1370
00:59:34.420 --> 00:59:38.980
You lure an otter with their own anal glands crushed up?

1371
00:59:39.140 --> 00:59:41.700
Yeah, with the scent of their own anal glands.

1372
00:59:42.380 --> 00:59:42.980
Why?

1373
00:59:43.520 --> 00:59:44.120
Exactly.

1374
00:59:44.260 --> 00:59:46.460
Why am I luring an otter?

1375
00:59:46.940 --> 00:59:48.920
I guess hunting.

1376
00:59:49.500 --> 00:59:53.200
But it's just for sport because you don't really use them for anything.

1377
00:59:53.400 --> 00:59:56.120
You don't use their fur or you don't eat them.

1378
00:59:56.380 --> 01:00:00.700
You just, I guess, sprinkle their anal glands and then kill them.

1379
01:00:00.900 --> 01:00:01.180
Why?

1380
01:00:01.700 --> 01:00:02.640
This is horrible.

1381
01:00:03.160 --> 01:00:07.940
Well, there's a whole market for this sort of like...

1382
01:00:07.940 --> 01:00:09.780
Surely there's something we don't know.

1383
01:00:10.020 --> 01:00:10.980
I don't know.

1384
01:00:10.980 --> 01:00:14.400
But there's like a lot of gland...

1385
01:00:14.760 --> 01:00:17.180
You can buy a lot of glands of different animals

1386
01:00:17.180 --> 01:00:19.160
for this purpose of luring them.

1387
01:00:19.540 --> 01:00:23.060
So basically, these animals smell their own...

1388
01:00:23.060 --> 01:00:25.460
And I guess they think it's another otter

1389
01:00:25.460 --> 01:00:26.760
and they come out to say hi.

1390
01:00:27.400 --> 01:00:27.880
Terrible.

1391
01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:30.300
This is like Bambi all over again for me.

1392
01:00:30.420 --> 01:00:30.860
I know.

1393
01:00:31.580 --> 01:00:36.040
And no references to rubbing it into someone's back for an injury.

1394
01:00:36.540 --> 01:00:39.080
I just want to say, if Phyllis went out to the woods,

1395
01:00:39.260 --> 01:00:40.440
she would be very popular.

1396
01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:42.120
It'd be like a Snow White moment for her.

1397
01:00:42.120 --> 01:00:45.060
She would have a lot of otters coming to say hi.

1398
01:00:45.640 --> 01:00:47.540
So Dwight and the Shroots could kill them.

1399
01:00:47.660 --> 01:00:48.560
I guess so.

1400
01:00:48.720 --> 01:00:51.540
But that is some information I found.

1401
01:00:51.740 --> 01:00:53.700
I'm confused by all of that information.

1402
01:00:53.700 --> 01:00:55.520
But thank you for sharing it.

1403
01:00:55.680 --> 01:00:56.160
You're welcome.

1404
01:00:56.500 --> 01:00:56.880
Okay.

1405
01:00:57.400 --> 01:01:00.720
There is a full-on dance competition happening now.

1406
01:01:01.020 --> 01:01:02.220
There sure is.

1407
01:01:02.360 --> 01:01:03.360
And we got mail.

1408
01:01:03.540 --> 01:01:04.960
From Alexis W.

1409
01:01:05.160 --> 01:01:06.680
in Australia who said,

1410
01:01:06.680 --> 01:01:12.620
I have to know if the dance competition between Andy and Kelly was freestyle or scripted.

1411
01:01:13.020 --> 01:01:14.840
Lady, I went to the script.

1412
01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:15.300
Mm-hmm.

1413
01:01:15.520 --> 01:01:16.320
And guess what?

1414
01:01:16.480 --> 01:01:18.460
It is fully scripted.

1415
01:01:18.700 --> 01:01:20.520
I'm going to read you the descriptions.

1416
01:01:21.180 --> 01:01:25.360
Here is Andy's dance-off description of the script.

1417
01:01:25.880 --> 01:01:28.860
Andy takes a chair and hops over the back of it,

1418
01:01:29.160 --> 01:01:31.980
turns around, does a fret of stare, walk over it

1419
01:01:31.980 --> 01:01:34.140
so that the chair's back falls to the ground.

1420
01:01:34.140 --> 01:01:41.080
He picks it up, sits on it, and spins on it in a Bob Fosse fashion, flashing jazz hands.

1421
01:01:41.340 --> 01:01:45.140
He then throws one leg over the back of the chair, gets up, picks up the chair,

1422
01:01:45.220 --> 01:01:47.420
and pretends that the chair is attacking him,

1423
01:01:47.480 --> 01:01:50.020
and then pretends to be killed by the chair.

1424
01:01:50.160 --> 01:01:54.520
He gets up and eyes Kelly like, try to top that.

1425
01:01:55.920 --> 01:01:57.500
Kelly's description is this.

1426
01:01:57.820 --> 01:02:00.100
Kelly walks over to the shower area.

1427
01:02:00.100 --> 01:02:05.560
She pretends to turn on the shower and mimes getting wet and lathering herself with soap.

1428
01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:06.320
Oh, my.

1429
01:02:06.540 --> 01:02:10.060
She then walks to the chair, which is next to where Andy stands.

1430
01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:14.860
She stops at the chair, turns like a runway model, winks at Andy,

1431
01:02:15.160 --> 01:02:18.400
picks up the chair, and walks back to the shower area with it.

1432
01:02:18.580 --> 01:02:24.420
She sits down, arches her back a la Flashdance, and pretends to be drenched by the shower.

1433
01:02:24.420 --> 01:02:27.520
She whips her hair around her head in a frenzy.

1434
01:02:27.840 --> 01:02:30.140
Then Kelly starts crunking.

1435
01:02:30.580 --> 01:02:30.800
Okay.

1436
01:02:31.100 --> 01:02:32.420
That's a dance move.

1437
01:02:32.580 --> 01:02:34.360
She's putting all her best stuff out there.

1438
01:02:34.540 --> 01:02:35.260
She is.

1439
01:02:35.820 --> 01:02:41.740
Well, you might notice that the way it is described is not exactly how you end up seeing it in the show,

1440
01:02:41.780 --> 01:02:46.340
and that is because Randy did hire a choreographer,

1441
01:02:46.760 --> 01:02:49.420
and the choreographer's job was to interpret the script

1442
01:02:49.420 --> 01:02:53.840
and then work with the actors to come up with their moves.

1443
01:02:54.040 --> 01:02:57.400
Randy said our choreographer's name was Mary Ann Kellogg,

1444
01:02:57.540 --> 01:03:01.740
and she came to us via recommendation from our first AD, Kelly Cantley.

1445
01:03:02.220 --> 01:03:05.880
Randy said she was absolutely superb and really got this scene.

1446
01:03:06.100 --> 01:03:08.880
And Warren said they had to use a good choreographer

1447
01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:12.020
because if the actors had just freestyled the whole time,

1448
01:03:12.240 --> 01:03:14.640
it would have been impossible to edit.

1449
01:03:15.020 --> 01:03:18.640
So although Mindy and Ed brought a lot of ideas for what they would do,

1450
01:03:18.640 --> 01:03:21.220
this was a planned out dance-off.

1451
01:03:21.560 --> 01:03:22.000
Yeah.

1452
01:03:22.240 --> 01:03:25.880
Sort of based loosely on the script, it seeMs.

1453
01:03:26.240 --> 01:03:27.040
But it morphed.

1454
01:03:27.080 --> 01:03:30.000
It evolved in this rehearsal process.

1455
01:03:30.380 --> 01:03:35.220
And Halstead said he definitely remembers them pitching that flash dance moment,

1456
01:03:35.820 --> 01:03:37.200
and that made it in.

1457
01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:39.700
You know, something else that Randy shared with us

1458
01:03:39.700 --> 01:03:44.020
was that we were not listening to music during this.

1459
01:03:44.140 --> 01:03:46.420
They were using what's called a thump track.

1460
01:03:46.420 --> 01:03:48.660
Again, for editing purposes,

1461
01:03:49.120 --> 01:03:51.720
they would just have this speaker that would play

1462
01:03:51.720 --> 01:03:55.420
like the sort of baseline beat of the song

1463
01:03:56.120 --> 01:03:58.360
so that the actors would be on tempo.

1464
01:03:59.480 --> 01:04:02.720
But for editing purposes, they put the song on later.

1465
01:04:03.000 --> 01:04:03.460
I know.

1466
01:04:03.560 --> 01:04:05.100
So these guys are doing this whole thing.

1467
01:04:05.220 --> 01:04:05.660
You were there.

1468
01:04:05.760 --> 01:04:06.540
Was it so crazy?

1469
01:04:06.760 --> 01:04:07.700
It was so crazy.

1470
01:04:07.700 --> 01:04:10.840
And also, this is not about the dance-off,

1471
01:04:10.840 --> 01:04:15.340
but I could always really tell when there was no music when Ellie was dancing.

1472
01:04:16.480 --> 01:04:20.600
Because Ellie just kind of does these jabs and kind of runs in place.

1473
01:04:20.780 --> 01:04:23.320
Like, not at all to what the beat of the song was that's playing.

1474
01:04:23.460 --> 01:04:24.940
But there was one little moment.

1475
01:04:25.080 --> 01:04:27.780
In the background, I start to tidy up.

1476
01:04:28.120 --> 01:04:28.560
Yes.

1477
01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:30.900
And Michael tells Angela to stop.

1478
01:04:31.180 --> 01:04:34.000
And this cracked me up, Jenna, because I do this in real life.

1479
01:04:34.440 --> 01:04:37.240
I often, at a party at a total stranger's house,

1480
01:04:37.420 --> 01:04:40.340
will start stacking plates, busting dirty cups,

1481
01:04:40.640 --> 01:04:43.440
putting napkins in the trash bin, you know?

1482
01:04:43.620 --> 01:04:46.700
Well, this is where we're similar because I do the same thing.

1483
01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:47.200
Yeah.

1484
01:04:47.560 --> 01:04:49.060
You know, someone did that to me once.

1485
01:04:49.160 --> 01:04:50.160
Like, I had thrown a party,

1486
01:04:50.780 --> 01:04:55.080
and one of the moms went around and was, like, tidying kind of near the end.

1487
01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:57.280
And I was, like, oh, my gosh.

1488
01:04:57.320 --> 01:05:00.360
That just took up, like, 25% of the cleaning.

1489
01:05:00.620 --> 01:05:02.240
And it was such a nice gesture.

1490
01:05:02.720 --> 01:05:04.500
So now I like to do it, too.

1491
01:05:04.640 --> 01:05:08.300
When I leave a party, I'll first do a sweep.

1492
01:05:08.460 --> 01:05:08.860
Yes.

1493
01:05:08.980 --> 01:05:11.560
And get rid of all the empty cans and glasses and things.

1494
01:05:11.560 --> 01:05:12.820
Stack the plastic cups.

1495
01:05:12.940 --> 01:05:13.180
Yes.

1496
01:05:13.280 --> 01:05:14.620
Put stuff by the sink.

1497
01:05:14.760 --> 01:05:15.040
Mm-hmm.

1498
01:05:15.360 --> 01:05:15.780
Mm-hmm.

1499
01:05:15.900 --> 01:05:16.320
I know.

1500
01:05:16.580 --> 01:05:18.260
I related to Angela in that moment.

1501
01:05:18.380 --> 01:05:21.460
But I love how she's, like, am I not supposed to have any fun?

1502
01:05:21.600 --> 01:05:23.580
Yes, because tidying is fun for her.

1503
01:05:24.360 --> 01:05:26.420
We should probably check back in on Phyllis.

1504
01:05:26.860 --> 01:05:27.140
Yeah.

1505
01:05:27.760 --> 01:05:31.080
Dwight is still trying to work out this kink in her back.

1506
01:05:31.220 --> 01:05:32.740
And then Phyllis just blurts out,

1507
01:05:32.920 --> 01:05:35.040
I think Bob's going to cheat on me with his new secretary.

1508
01:05:35.480 --> 01:05:36.820
And then she starts laughing.

1509
01:05:37.080 --> 01:05:38.700
And then her back is better.

1510
01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:39.460
Yeah.

1511
01:05:39.860 --> 01:05:41.460
And Dwight's like, why are you laughing?

1512
01:05:41.460 --> 01:05:43.780
And she said, oh, just saying it out loud.

1513
01:05:43.880 --> 01:05:45.180
It just sounds so ridiculous.

1514
01:05:45.440 --> 01:05:48.500
And she was able to let all that tension and stress go.

1515
01:05:48.780 --> 01:05:52.440
We had a fan question from Liz B in Columbus, Ohio.

1516
01:05:52.440 --> 01:05:58.500
